1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,600
 The NCSA Mosaic web browser was developed at the University of Illinois starting in 1992.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,560
 NCSA Mosaic brought the web to Apple, PC, and Unix systems.

3
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:15,960
 Joseph Hardin managed the software development group at the National Center for Supercomputing

4
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:18,880
 Applications.

5
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:25,560
 NCSA was kind of a special place, right?

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:31,600
 One of those things that happens very infrequently where there's lots of energy, a large amount

7
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:37,960
 of resources, visionary leadership, and a lot of free space for people to play.

8
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:43,180
 And Larry Smarr has to figure in any discussion of it quickly because he's the person that

9
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,560
 created that space.

10
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:54,320
 And that gave a bunch of us, people in visualization, people in computational science, people in

11
00:00:54,320 --> 00:01:00,120
 collaboration technologies, people in all kinds of stuff, networking.

12
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:05,960
 In the middle and late 80s, a space to just figure out what it was that we thought was

13
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:07,540
 fun and what was interesting.

14
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:12,800
 What I thought was interesting was how people were using these new technologies to work

15
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:13,800
 together.

16
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:25,480
 And we started out working with tools that supported simulation and computations on the

17
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,840
 main supercomputing systems.

18
00:01:28,840 --> 00:01:31,320
 That's what the software development group did.

19
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:37,480
 And there was a group before I joined it that did NCSA Telnet, right?

20
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:43,160
 This is back when we were trying to convince the physicists that DECnet wasn't such a great

21
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:52,240
 idea, that we wanted this crazy, open, nobody owns it, right, IP model for the network.

22
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:59,840
 And that was kind of a bit of a struggle because if it was an open source type thing and nobody

23
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:06,120
 owns it, how's anybody going to have responsibility for it and why not use DECnet, which is this

24
00:02:06,120 --> 00:02:12,000
 perfectly good proprietary, well-developed system, yadda yadda.

25
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,480
 So it took a while for us to explain to the physicists why we wanted an open model for

26
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:23,160
 TCP/IP, for the interconnection of all these networks.

27
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,960
 And I spent time for a while with a program at NCSA called the Affiliates Program, where

28
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:38,280
 we gave people Mac SEs and told them about TCP and put the NCSA Telnet on the machine

29
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:41,080
 and said, "Now, here's how you get to the supercomputers."

30
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:46,960
 Larry recognized from the beginning, and we all loved the idea, that these small little

31
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:52,000
 things on the desktop were really the gateways for everybody to the big machines in the background.

32
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,840
 And that all of this would turn into one cloud behind the screen that we needed to figure

33
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:00,280
 out how to get the user involved in as much as possible.

34
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:05,240
 So we were interested in building tools for end users that worked with PCs and that allowed

35
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:10,440
 them to do their work better in the computational science communities that they were part of

36
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:12,940
 on the supercomputers.

37
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:18,120
 And it's an easy extension from that to think about collaborative technologies in the large.

38
00:03:18,120 --> 00:03:28,400
 How do people work together, not only with these tools, but also with simple communications,

39
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:36,200
 email, papers, data sets that they want to share, and how do they do that?

40
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:42,280
 Initially, our interest was in synchronous tools when we were thinking of collaboration

41
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:43,280
 tools.

42
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:50,720
 So we were building something called NCSA Collage, which was a set of tools that worked,

43
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:56,120
 and one of the big deals was that they worked across the three platforms, the X Windows

44
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:02,240
 for the UNIX people, the Windows environment, and the Mac.

45
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:07,520
 And that was one of the things that was sort of part of the underlying culture there was

46
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:10,880
 we wanted to make them available to as large a community as possible.

47
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:16,280
 So we started working on collaborative tools, and there was a set of people on each of those

48
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:22,440
 machines that was working to make it possible for people to share in real time images of

49
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:30,260
 their data, the spreadsheets of their data, and papers that they had run across interesting

50
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:35,440
 references to with their colleagues who were remote from them geographically.

51
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:40,560
 So that's the context in which Dave Thompson, who was one of the developers, one of the

52
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:48,920
 X Windows developers, the lead X Windows developer, I think, for the Collage tool, pulled down

53
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:53,220
 one of the early web browsers.

54
00:04:53,220 --> 00:05:00,200
 It was the one from Slack, and I can't remember its name, and he went through the effort of

55
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:06,120
 getting it working and brought it in and showed it to Mark Andreessen and I.

56
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:12,080
 And both of us looked at the screen, Dave described what he had in front of us, and

57
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:15,840
 we said, "We can do better than that.

58
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:19,580
 That's a complicated system, and the interface looks terrible."

59
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:25,880
 And Dave said that it was a real pain for him to get it working, for him to download

60
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:29,200
 it and install it and compile it and everything.

61
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:34,960
 And it only works on an X Windows box, and wouldn't it be cool if it worked across all

62
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:38,640
 three of the boxes and if it was something that was just a plug-and-go like the rest

63
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,000
 of our tools?

64
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,840
 So Mark and another developer there ran with the idea.

65
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:50,280
 Dave wanted to work on it, but I said, "Dave, please finish up with the Collage stuff."

66
00:05:50,280 --> 00:05:57,020
 And this was before we really understood what was meant by open source, but we wanted everybody

67
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:59,960
 to be able to take the software and do whatever they wanted to with it.

68
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:02,520
 We weren't that concerned with commercial advantage.

69
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:08,660
 We were more interested in it being open and people being able to make contributions back

70
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:12,080
 to the code and taking the code and doing what they wanted to with it.

71
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:18,280
 So we just put it all in the public domain, which as the folks from Apache will tell you,

72
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:19,280
 was kind of ambiguous.

73
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:24,740
 It's not clear what that means, but at least it got the code out there.

74
00:06:24,740 --> 00:06:27,280
 So Mark and Eric were working on that.

75
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:30,100
 Mark and Eric were working on what?

76
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:36,640
 The first generation of NCSA Mosaic, and this was an X Windows app.

77
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:41,040
 And nobody was working yet on the Windows or the Mac version.

78
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:44,400
 And we saw the first version of that come out when was it?

79
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:49,280
 Early '93, late '92, and it was probably early '93.

80
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:53,160
 And the response, of course, was fantastic.

81
00:06:53,160 --> 00:06:59,080
 It's wonderful to be able to just click on something and see it right away.

82
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:08,800
 And indeed, the combination of hyperlinks in a document as far as navigation and retrieval

83
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:13,240
 of documents as a user interface is just great.

84
00:07:13,240 --> 00:07:20,840
 And a lot of people got it immediately, especially people that were working with the tools at

85
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:27,240
 NCSA and the companies-- I remember an HP exec coming in one time, and Mark and Eric

86
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:36,440
 had written a little filter that took Unix documentation and made it into HTML and made

87
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:39,160
 all of the references into links.

88
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:44,520
 And they went to-- they hit an HP site, and the exec said, "Where's this coming from?"

89
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:49,120
 Because he was able to see all of his HP documentation there in the room at NCSA and navigate through

90
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:50,120
 it really easily.

91
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:55,760
 And he said, "Well, you've got three or four folks back there that have put up HTTPD servers.

92
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,040
 You may not know what those are yet."

93
00:07:57,040 --> 00:07:58,640
 And he said, "I've never heard of this."

94
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:02,480
 And we said, "But this is the kind of thing that is probably going to be really useful

95
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:06,840
 in the future for people who are trying to manage documentation in a distributed fashion."

96
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:07,840
 Yada, yada, yada.

97
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:08,840
 Right?

98
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:11,960
 We went on with a story like that, and this guy was bouncing up and down in his seat.

99
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:16,600
 And this was the kind of response that we got with it.

100
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:23,360
 For a while, we tried to integrate the earlier work with Collage with the work with Mosaic.

101
00:08:23,360 --> 00:08:27,480
 And early versions of Mosaic have a Collaborate button at the top.

102
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:33,120
 And that was something that would allow you to pull in something from a synchronous session.

103
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:37,900
 The idea was that you'd be working with your colleagues synchronously, and then asynchronously

104
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:46,960
 go off and use the browser and pull something into the Collage session, or vice versa, be

105
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:51,300
 working on something in the Collage session and be able to access it through the browser,

106
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:55,320
 or just use the browser as a component in the session.

107
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:59,800
 Well, the browser, of course, took off.

108
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:11,500
 We had Tom Redmond, who was the lead for the Mac version, though he was the person who

109
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:18,800
 really built it was Alex Todit, who was an excellent developer and a lot of times ran

110
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:23,760
 ahead of the other people that were working on stuff, especially on the Mac.

111
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:27,520
 And Chris Wilson and John, they were working on the Windows version.

112
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:38,280
 So all of a sudden, in '93, late '93, early '94, there's this full suite of Mosaics that

113
00:09:38,280 --> 00:09:43,720
 work across the X Windows, the Mac, and the Windows system.

114
00:09:43,720 --> 00:09:51,240
 And that's the point at which the guy who was the president at the time of the Internet

115
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:56,760
 Association said NCSA has fired a shot heard around the world, because it's available now

116
00:09:56,760 --> 00:10:00,960
 across all these platforms and anybody can use it.

117
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:06,980
 We were convinced deep down inside that all of the new technologies and the digitalization

118
00:10:06,980 --> 00:10:10,320
 of the world and everything was going to make a huge difference.

119
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:11,920
 We just weren't sure exactly how.

120
00:10:11,920 --> 00:10:19,360
 At the same time, when people would come to us early on in the Mosaic experience and say,

121
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:24,400
 we want to commercialize this and do this with it or do that with it, there was a lot

122
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:25,920
 of, you know, we're not sure.

123
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:32,840
 I remember sitting in rooms with commercial folks that came in and pitched to us.

124
00:10:32,840 --> 00:10:38,480
 And I'd bring the whole team in, the whole software development group, and say, listen

125
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:43,280
 to these guys and, you know, tell me what you think about this.

126
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:46,260
 And nobody was sure what it was going to do.

127
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:52,640
 And there were people who started off building browsers and just kind of never got off the

128
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:53,640
 ground.

129
00:10:53,640 --> 00:11:02,720
 Until the Netscape effort started up, there was sufficient energy and sufficient resources,

130
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:15,680
 I think, to really get on and ride and push and, you know, crank up a group of X hundred

131
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:19,320
 developers in a matter of months.

132
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:25,880
 And then they were, you know, quickly overshadowed by the effort that Microsoft put into it.

133
00:11:25,880 --> 00:11:31,640
 I remember one of the Netscape guys saying, I came back from a meeting with some folks

134
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:37,600
 up in Seattle, and they said that Microsoft now had, this is when Netscape was riding

135
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:39,800
 at the top of its form, right?

136
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:41,200
 Top of the game.

137
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:46,000
 This guy says, Microsoft just told me that there's somebody from Microsoft just said

138
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,800
 that they've got like 2,000 developers working on this.

139
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:55,200
 He said, at that point, I realized that we were, you know, going to have some difficulties.

140
00:11:55,200 --> 00:12:02,600
 And we, of course, always felt that there should be more than one browser.

141
00:12:02,600 --> 00:12:06,840
 And so we wanted this because we were interested in standards and openness right at the time.

142
00:12:06,840 --> 00:12:11,160
 If there's only one browser, then that company gets to determine what the standards are.

143
00:12:11,160 --> 00:12:16,080
 And there were all kinds of hassles early on about putting in different features and

144
00:12:16,080 --> 00:12:19,360
 the browsers driving the standards rather than the standards driving the browsers and

145
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:20,440
 all this kind of stuff.

146
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:22,700
 So we wanted some diversity.

147
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:29,160
 There was a feeling very early on that this was going to be a real gas.

148
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:34,860
 This was going to be hot, that this was something that was, the response was just so immediate.

149
00:12:34,860 --> 00:12:43,880
 If you go back and you ask people who were sitting in front of machines in 1993, 94,

150
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:48,760
 95, right, and you say, do you remember the first time that you used Mosaic?

151
00:12:48,760 --> 00:12:49,760
 Right?

152
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,800
 Or do you remember the first time you used a browser that wasn't Mosaic?

153
00:12:52,800 --> 00:12:58,160
 And vast majority of them that I've run into say, yeah, I can remember.

154
00:12:58,160 --> 00:13:03,080
 And I remember one of the NSF program officers calling me up one day just out of the blue

155
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:08,160
 and saying, I just wanted to tell you, you know, you guys, this was like after we'd been

156
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:14,760
 playing with this for months, right, not years, just months, he said, you know, sometimes

157
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:21,020
 we just sit around and click on things because it's still such a surprise to have a picture

158
00:13:21,020 --> 00:13:24,940
 or a whole nother page of information pop up, right?

159
00:13:24,940 --> 00:13:28,620
 And we don't know where it's coming from and we don't know who's putting it up, but we

160
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:29,760
 We still haven't gotten over

161
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:33,200
 the gee whiz aspect of this yet.

